  "content": {
    "id": "p3-q6-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "6",
    "prompt": "Find points $P(x,y)$ on the graph of $y=x^2$ given the distance from the origin to $P$ is $\\sqrt{30}$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$(\\sqrt{5},5)$ and $(-\\sqrt{5},5)$.",
      "worked": "Using the distance from the origin and $y=x^2$,\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2+y^2&=30\\\\x^2+(x^2)^2&=30\\\\x^4+x^2-30&=0\\\\(x^2+6)(x^2-5)&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $x^2\\geq0$, the factor $x^2+6$ cannot be zero. Therefore,\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2&=5\\\\x&=\\pm\\sqrt{5}\\\\y&=5.\\end{align*}$$\nThe points are $(\\sqrt{5},5)$ and $(-\\sqrt{5},5)$. Each satisfies $y=x^2$ and has distance $\\sqrt{5+25}=\\sqrt{30}$ from the origin.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 64
  },
